{"Images": [{"Name": "i0", "Description": "Diabetic nephropathy (DN)", "Path": "images/DN-arteryV2", "Width": 1405, "Height": 1324, "MaxLevel": 1}], "Header": "**Diabetic nephropathy** is one of the most commun causes of chronic kidney disease. The glomerulus is its primary target. Early changes include glomerular hyperfiltration and progressive podocyte injury. \n\nPatients with diabetic nephropathy often have concomitant vascular disease and a high cardiovascular risk, making them a relevant comparator for patients with CCE-AKI.", "Rotation": 0, "Layout": {"Grid": [["i0"]]}, "Stories": [{"Name": "", "Description": "", "Waypoints": [{"Name": "Structure of the artery", "Description": "The renal artery plays a critical role in maintaining kidney perfusion and delivering oxygen and nutrients to renal tissues. Its vascular wall is composed of endothelial cells lining the lumen and smooth muscle cells that regulate blood flow. ", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Artery", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.4429687500000001, "Pan": [0.5305891238670694, 0.4539517455735279]}, {"Name": "Complement activation", "Description": "The complement system is a key component of innate immunity and can be activated through three pathways: the classical, lectin, and alternative pathways. Here, complement activation is barely detectable, with only C3 (detected using an anti-C3d antibody) and C9 deposits observed within the arterial wall, but not along the endothelium.", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Complement activation", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.4270312500000001, "Pan": [0.5305891238670696, 0.4539517455735279]}, {"Name": "Immune infiltration", "Description": "Periarterial immune infiltration is minimal, with only a few macrophages detected.", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Immune cells", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.4942491319444446, "Pan": [0.20601356918027672, 0.4540671628097231]}]}], "Channels": [{"Rendered": false, "Na